==English==
{{was wotd|2009|August|14}}

===Etymology===
From {{compound|en|eaves|drop}}; the "listening" sense derives from the notion of the listener standing in the area denoted by the physical sense.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈiːvzˌdɹɒp/|a=UK}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈivzˌdɹɑp/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-eavesdrop.ogg|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-au-eavesdrop.ogg|a=AU}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|++}}

# {{senseid|en|to hear a conversation one is not intended to hear}} {{lb|en|ambitransitive}} To [[hear]] ([[intentionally]]) a [[conversation]] one is not intended to hear; to [[listen in]].
#: {{ux|en|I hope nobody was '''eavesdropping''' on our conversation last night, since it got so personal.}}
# {{lb|en|zoology}} To listen for another [[organism]]'s [[call]]s, so as to [[exploit]] them.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1993|author=David W. Nagorsen; Robert Mark Brigham; Royal British Columbia Museum|title=Bats of British Columbia|page=109
|passage={{...}} Eastern Red Bat will '''eavesdrop''' on the sonar calls of other red bats to locate potential insect prey.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2014|author=Ken Yasukawa|title=Animal Behavior: How and Why Animals Do the Things They Do|passage=Frog-biting midges also depend on '''eavesdropping''' for reproduction, and one would predict similarly strong selection to evolve auditory adaptations for hearing and localizing calls of their hosts.}}

====Usage notes====
To ''eavesdrop'' usually implies the listener is purposefully trying to hear the conversation of others. To {{m|en|overhear}} is more often accidental.

====Hypernyms====
* {{sense|hear conversation one is not intended to hear}} {{l|en|overhear}}

